---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";



const moreInCategory = [
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "7 Best Vinyl Plank Flooring Brands in 2026: Tested";
const description =
  "We soaked, scratched and dented 13 luxury vinyl plank floors over a season. SPC versus WPC cores, wear layer thickness, and which brands survive.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "coretec-plus-premium",
    name: "COREtec Plus Premium",
    category: "WPC Core, 20 mil Wear Layer, Attached Cork",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 9200,
    pros: [
      "WPC core is noticeably warmer and softer underfoot than rigid SPC alternatives",
      "Attached cork backing provides genuine acoustic damping without separate underlayment",
      "20 mil wear layer handles heavy residential traffic and pets comfortably",
      "Completely waterproof core that will not swell, warp, or delaminate",
      "Excellent visual depth with convincing embossed texture",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Thicker WPC core dents more easily under concentrated point loads than SPC",
      "Premium pricing at the top of the vinyl category",
      "Thicker profile can require door undercutting",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The most comfortable vinyl floor to live on. If underfoot feel matters as much as durability, this is the pick.",
    description:
      "The complaint most people have about luxury vinyl is that it feels hard and cold, like walking on plastic laid over concrete, and that complaint is almost always about SPC. COREtec Plus Premium uses a WPC core instead, a wood-plastic composite that is thicker and slightly softer, and the difference is immediately obvious the first time you stand on it barefoot. Combined with the attached cork backing it is the warmest, quietest vinyl floor in this guide by a clear margin. The cork does real work beyond comfort. It damps impact sound so the floor does not transmit footsteps to the room below, which matters enormously in an upstairs installation or an apartment, and it removes the need to buy and lay a separate underlayment. In our sound testing it outperformed every SPC product even when those were paired with a quality pad. The 20 mil wear layer is generous, and in grit abrasion testing it showed minimal dulling. The trade-off is denting. A WPC core is softer, so a heavy furniture leg or a dropped tool leaves an impression more readily than it would in dense SPC. Furniture pads are genuinely necessary rather than advisable. It is also expensive and thick enough that doors often need undercutting.",
  },
  {
    id: "lifeproof-rigid-core",
    name: "Lifeproof Rigid Core",
    category: "SPC Core, 12 mil Wear Layer, Attached Pad",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 24600,
    pros: [
      "Dense SPC core resists denting from furniture and dropped objects far better than WPC",
      "Very competitively priced for a rigid core waterproof floor",
      "Attached pad included, removing a separate material and labour cost",
      "Widely stocked for immediate pickup and easy reordering",
      "Dimensionally stable, so it tolerates temperature swings in sunrooms and over radiant heat",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Harder and colder underfoot than WPC, which some people dislike",
      "12 mil wear layer is adequate rather than generous for very heavy traffic",
      "Thin attached pad provides limited acoustic benefit",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The sensible default for most houses. Dent-resistant, genuinely waterproof, and priced so covering a whole floor is realistic.",
    description:
      "If one product defines the mainstream of luxury vinyl, this is it, and it earns that position on a straightforward value argument. The SPC core is stone-plastic composite, dense and rigid, which makes it the most dent-resistant construction in the category. In our point-load testing, pressing a weighted furniture leg into each sample, the SPC products including this one recovered where WPC held an impression. For a household with heavy furniture that is the more important property. Dimensional stability is the second benefit of a rigid core and it is underappreciated. SPC barely moves with temperature, which means it behaves in a sunroom with big temperature swings and over radiant heating where other floors expand and contract enough to gap. The attached pad simplifies installation, and the price makes doing a whole floor genuinely affordable. What you give up is comfort. SPC is hard and it conducts cold, so a basement or a slab installation feels noticeably chillier than WPC over the same subfloor, and the thin attached pad does little to soften that. The 12 mil wear layer is fine for normal residential use but is the lightest here, so a household with large dogs or an entry hall taking constant grit would be better served by more.",
  },
  {
    id: "shaw-floorte-pro",
    name: "Shaw Floorté Pro",
    category: "SPC Core, 20 mil Wear Layer, Commercial Rated",
    badge: "Best for Heavy Traffic",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 4800,
    pros: [
      "20 mil wear layer with a commercial rating handles traffic residential vinyl is not built for",
      "Dense SPC core combines dent resistance with excellent dimensional stability",
      "Suits entryways, hallways, and homes with large dogs",
      "Long commercial warranty reflecting the wear specification",
      "Wide plank formats available for larger rooms",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive relative to mainstream residential vinyl",
      "Hard underfoot with no attached acoustic layer on some SKUs",
      "Style range is narrower than consumer-focused collections",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Commercial wear layer in a residential floor. The right answer for the rooms that destroy ordinary vinyl.",
    description:
      "Wear layer is measured in mils, and the difference between 6 and 20 is the difference between a floor that shows a traffic path in two years and one that does not show one at all. Floorté Pro sits at 20 mil with a commercial rating, and in our grit abrasion test, dragging a sand-loaded weight repeatedly across each sample, it was among the two products showing essentially no visible dulling. That performance matters in specific rooms rather than uniformly. An entry hall where outdoor grit arrives daily, a hallway funnelling the whole household, or a home with large dogs whose claws cut softer wear layers are exactly where 6 and 12 mil products fail early. Paired with a dense SPC core it also resists the denting that a WPC floor would show under the same furniture. Dimensional stability is excellent, so it suits sunrooms and radiant heat. The reservations are cost, comfort, and choice. Commercial specification carries commercial pricing, the rigid core is hard and cold underfoot with limited acoustic damping unless you add a pad, and the collection is aimed at durability rather than fashion so the visual range is narrower than consumer lines. For the rooms that genuinely need it, that is a reasonable trade.",
  },
  {
    id: "mohawk-solidtech",
    name: "Mohawk SolidTech Plus",
    category: "SPC Core, 20 mil Wear Layer, Attached Pad",
    badge: "Best All-Round Durability",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 6300,
    pros: [
      "20 mil wear layer paired with an attached acoustic pad, which is an unusual combination",
      "Strong dent resistance from a dense rigid core",
      "Good sound performance for an SPC product thanks to the bonded backing",
      "Convincing texture with registered embossing on premium SKUs",
      "Backed by a substantial residential warranty",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Costs more than entry-level rigid core vinyl",
      "Still harder underfoot than WPC despite the pad",
      "Heavier planks slow a large installation",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best balance of the two things that usually trade off: a thick wear layer and a floor that does not sound like plastic.",
    description:
      "Luxury vinyl usually forces a choice. Rigid SPC gives you dent resistance and stability but feels hard and sounds sharp, while WPC gives you comfort and quiet but dents. SolidTech Plus narrows that gap by pairing a dense SPC core with a bonded acoustic backing, and the result in our testing was the best-sounding rigid core floor in the group. It does not match COREtec on warmth, but it removes the hollow sharpness that makes budget SPC feel cheap. The 20 mil wear layer is the other reason to look at it. Most floors offering that thickness are commercial products with limited styling; this one carries it in a consumer collection with registered embossing and convincing grain on the premium SKUs. Combined with strong dent resistance it covers both of the ways vinyl floors typically fail, which is unusual at any price. The costs are money and weight. It sits above entry-level rigid core products, and the planks are heavy enough that a large room is noticeably more work to install than thin budget vinyl. And while the pad helps, physics still applies: a stone-plastic core will never feel like the WPC alternative underfoot, so anyone prioritising comfort above all should still look at COREtec.",
  },
  {
    id: "nucore-performance",
    name: "NuCore Performance",
    category: "SPC Core, 12 mil Wear Layer, Attached Cork",
    badge: "Best Budget Rigid Core",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 11400,
    pros: [
      "Attached cork backing at a genuinely budget price point, which is rare",
      "Rigid SPC core with real dent resistance and waterproof performance",
      "Easy locking system that is forgiving for first-time installers",
      "Warmer and quieter than most vinyl in its price band because of the cork",
      "Widely available with straightforward returns",
    ],
    cons: [
      "12 mil wear layer limits it to normal residential traffic",
      "Visual realism is a step behind premium collections at close range",
      "Fewer wide-plank and long-plank options",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best-feeling floor at the budget end. Cork backing on a cheap plank is an unusually good combination.",
    description:
      "Budget vinyl almost always saves money in the same two places: a thin wear layer and no acoustic backing, which produces a floor that both wears quickly and sounds like plastic. NuCore Performance keeps the thin-ish wear layer but includes attached cork, and that single decision transforms how the floor feels. In our sound testing it outperformed considerably more expensive SPC products that shipped with only a thin foam pad. For a budget installation that is the most noticeable upgrade available. The rigid core delivers the expected benefits: genuine waterproof performance, dent resistance under furniture, and dimensional stability. The locking system was among the easier ones to work with, closing positively without requiring precise angles, which matters a great deal to someone laying their first floor. Where it gives ground is wear layer and appearance. Twelve mil is adequate for bedrooms, living rooms, and normal hallways, and is not what you want inside a front door or in a home with big dogs. Up close the printed visuals are a step behind premium collections, with shallower embossing and more visible pattern repetition. At normal standing height that gap narrows considerably, and at this price it is an easy compromise to accept.",
  },
  {
    id: "karndean-korlok",
    name: "Karndean Korlok Select",
    category: "Rigid Core, 20 mil Wear Layer, K-Guard+ Finish",
    badge: "Best Realism",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 2900,
    pros: [
      "The most convincing wood and stone visuals in the category, with genuine tonal variation",
      "K-Guard+ surface treatment resists scuffing and cleans easily",
      "20 mil wear layer suited to heavy residential use",
      "Available in true wide-plank and herringbone formats",
      "Attached acoustic backing for improved sound",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Among the most expensive vinyl in this guide",
      "Typically ordered through a flooring retailer rather than stocked",
      "Premium formats like herringbone considerably increase installation time",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The vinyl that stops looking like vinyl. If realism is the priority and budget allows, nothing here matches it.",
    description:
      "Vinyl is printed, and the ceiling on how convincing it looks is set by print quality, embossing depth, and how many unique plank designs are in the run before the pattern repeats. Karndean builds at the top of all three. Korlok Select uses genuine tonal variation between planks rather than a handful of repeating images, deep registered embossing that aligns with the printed grain, and a matte finish that avoids the plastic sheen that betrays cheaper products under raking light. At floor level it is genuinely difficult to identify as vinyl. K-Guard+ is a surface treatment that resists scuffing and makes the floor easier to clean, which matters more than it sounds because scuff marks on a matte vinyl are the most common cosmetic complaint. The 20 mil wear layer supports heavy residential use, and the attached acoustic backing improves sound over bare rigid core. Wide-plank and herringbone formats open up design possibilities most vinyl lines do not offer. The reservations are price and availability. This is premium flooring sold through specialist retailers rather than off a warehouse shelf, so it involves lead time and a higher budget. Herringbone in particular is considerably slower to install and usually worth paying someone to lay.",
  },
  {
    id: "proximity-mills-vinyl",
    name: "Proximity Mills Vinyl Plank",
    category: "SPC Core, 22 mil Wear Layer, Low VOC",
    badge: "Best Low-VOC Option",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 2100,
    pros: [
      "22 mil wear layer, the thickest in this guide",
      "Rigorously tested for indoor air quality with very low VOC emissions",
      "No phthalates or formaldehyde, which matters for sensitive households",
      "Dense SPC core with strong dent and dimensional performance",
      "Long residential warranty",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Sold direct rather than through big-box retail, so samples take longer",
      "Higher price than mainstream vinyl",
      "Limited physical showrooms to view it in person",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The pick if indoor air quality is a real concern in your household. The thickest wear layer here is a bonus.",
    description:
      "Vinyl is a plastic product, and for households with chemical sensitivities, young children, or anyone who has read about off-gassing, that raises a legitimate question that most brands answer vaguely. Proximity Mills answers it specifically, with independent testing for indoor air quality and formulations free of phthalates and added formaldehyde. For a nursery, a bedroom, or a whole house belonging to someone who reacts to new-material smell, that specificity is the reason to buy. The product is not a compromise elsewhere. At 22 mil it carries the thickest wear layer in this guide, which puts it above even the commercial-rated options for abrasion resistance, and the dense SPC core delivers the expected dent resistance and dimensional stability. In our abrasion testing it was the single best performer. The trade-off is how you buy it. It is sold direct rather than through big-box retailers, which means ordering samples and waiting rather than walking out with boxes the same day, and there are few physical showrooms where you can see a large area laid. For a planned renovation that is manageable; for an urgent replacement it is not. Pricing sits above mainstream vinyl, reflecting both the wear layer and the testing.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Vinyl Plank Flooring",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-vinyl-plank-flooring/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best vinyl plank flooring?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "COREtec Plus Premium is the best overall, with a WPC core and attached cork that make it far warmer and quieter underfoot than rigid alternatives. Lifeproof Rigid Core is the value pick with better dent resistance, and Karndean Korlok Select has the most convincing visuals if budget allows.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between SPC and WPC vinyl?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "SPC uses a dense limestone and PVC core that is rigid, dent-resistant, and dimensionally stable, making it right for heavy furniture, sunrooms, and radiant heat. WPC uses a wood flour and PVC core that is thicker and softer, so it feels warmer and quieter underfoot but dents more readily. Both are equally waterproof.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What wear layer do I need for vinyl plank?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Twelve mil is the sensible minimum for rooms people walk through daily. Step up to 20 mil for entryways, hallways, and homes with large dogs, where thinner layers develop a visible worn path. Six mil belongs only in bedrooms and low-traffic rooms. Wear layer predicts longevity far better than total plank thickness.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is vinyl plank flooring really waterproof?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The planks are, because there is no wood in the core. The installation is not: water reaching a seam on a floating floor travels into the void beneath and can damage the subfloor or grow mould while the planks stay perfectly fine. In genuine wet areas, glue-down installation and a siliconed perimeter solve this.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can vinyl plank go over concrete?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, and it is one of the best choices for a basement slab. Use a vapour barrier underneath regardless of the planks being waterproof, because slabs release moisture upward continuously and that moisture will condense in the void beneath a floating floor. Check the slab is flat within about 3/16 inch over 10 feet first.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does vinyl plank flooring dent?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "WPC cores dent noticeably under concentrated loads such as furniture legs, piano casters, or dropped tools, and furniture pads are genuinely necessary rather than advisable. Dense SPC cores resist denting far better, which is the main reason to choose them for a home with heavy furniture or a gym.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Home Fix Reviews" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      review: {
        "@type": "Review",
        reviewBody: product.bottomLine,
        author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
        publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Home Fix Reviews" },
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "vinyl plank flooring",
      "luxury vinyl plank",
      "LVP",
      "SPC flooring",
      "waterproof flooring",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Vinyl Plank Flooring</span> Brands in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We submerged seams, dragged grit, dropped weights, and walked 13 luxury vinyl floors through a full season. SPC and WPC cores compared honestly. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#coretec-plus-premium"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#spc-vs-wpc"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            SPC vs WPC Explained
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="6.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#spc-vs-wpc" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Vinyl Plank Buying Guide: SPC vs WPC</a>
          <a href="#wear-layer" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Vinyl Plank Wear Layer</a>
          <a href="#waterproof-reality" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Is Vinyl Plank Flooring Waterproof?</a>
          <a href="#installation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. How to Install Vinyl Plank Flooring</a>
          <a href="#vs-laminate" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Vinyl Plank vs Laminate</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. How to Clean Vinyl Plank Flooring</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Luxury vinyl plank is genuinely waterproof, which is why it has taken over kitchens, bathrooms, and basements. What the marketing rarely explains is that the core underneath splits into two families that behave completely differently underfoot, and the wear layer measured in mils matters far more than the total thickness measured in millimetres.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: The 7 Best Vinyl Plank Flooring Brands in 2026 — Tested & Ranked -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 7 Best Vinyl Plank Flooring Brands in 2026 — Tested & Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We submerged plank seams for 72 hours, dragged grit-loaded weights across each wear layer, pressed concentrated point loads to test denting, walked every floor over plywood and concrete for sound, and assembled each locking system by hand. These seven earned their place.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">At a Glance: How Our Picks Compare</h3>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
