        <!-- SECTION 2: Vinyl Plank Flooring Buying Guide: SPC vs WPC -->
        <div id="spc-vs-wpc" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Vinyl Plank Flooring Buying Guide: SPC vs WPC Core
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every luxury vinyl plank is built around a core, and modern products use one of two. They are equally waterproof and behave completely differently in every other respect, which makes this the first decision to settle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">SPC — Stone Plastic Composite</h3>

          <p>
            A dense core of limestone powder and PVC. It is rigid, heavy, and very hard, which gives it excellent resistance to denting from furniture legs and dropped objects, and outstanding dimensional stability so it barely moves with temperature. That stability makes it the right choice over radiant heat and in sunrooms.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">WPC — Wood Plastic Composite</h3>

          <p>
            A core of wood flour and PVC with a foaming agent, making it thicker, lighter, and slightly softer. It is noticeably warmer and quieter underfoot, which is the single biggest complaint people have about vinyl. The cost is denting: concentrated point loads leave impressions that SPC would shrug off.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">SPC or WPC: How to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            If you have heavy furniture, a home gym, a sunroom, or radiant heating, choose SPC. If comfort underfoot and quietness matter most, particularly on an upper floor or in a bedroom, choose WPC and commit to furniture pads. Neither is more waterproof than the other.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ignore Total Thickness as a Quality Signal</h3>

          <p>
            WPC planks are thicker than SPC planks by design, so comparing overall millimetres across the two types tells you nothing about quality. A 5mm SPC plank and an 8mm WPC plank can be equally good products. The specification that actually predicts durability is the wear layer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flexible LVP Is a Third, Older Category</h3>

          <p>
            Glue-down flexible vinyl planks without a rigid core still exist and are cheaper. They telegraph every subfloor imperfection, are far more demanding to install well, and are generally not what people mean today by luxury vinyl plank. Rigid core is the modern default for a reason.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers Don't Lie</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 mil</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Wear layer for heavy traffic and pets</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">6 mil</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Budget wear layer, light use only</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">100%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Waterproof, both SPC and WPC cores</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">1/4"</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Expansion gap floating vinyl still needs</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 3: Vinyl Plank Wear Layer -->
        <div id="wear-layer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Vinyl Plank Wear Layer: The Only Durability Number That Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            The wear layer is the clear protective film over the printed design, measured in mils where one mil is a thousandth of an inch. It is the single best predictor of how long a vinyl floor keeps looking new, and it is frequently buried in the specifications while total thickness is advertised on the front.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>6 mil</strong> is light residential. It belongs in bedrooms and spare rooms with minimal traffic, and it is what most very cheap vinyl uses. In an entry or hallway it develops a visible dull path within a couple of years.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>12 mil</strong> is standard residential and covers living rooms, dining rooms, and normal hallways for a typical household. It is the sensible minimum for anywhere people walk daily, and it is what most mainstream products offer.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>20 mil and above</strong> is heavy residential and light commercial. This is what handles entryways carrying outdoor grit, homes with large dogs, and rooms that see genuinely constant traffic. It is also what most commercial installations specify, which tells you something about its durability.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wear layer beats total thickness every time.</strong> A 5mm plank with a 20 mil wear layer will look new for far longer than an 8mm plank with a 6 mil layer, because wear happens at the surface. Thickness affects feel, sound, and how well the plank bridges subfloor flaws — not longevity.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Match it to the room rather than the house.</strong> Running 20 mil in the entry and hallway with 12 mil in bedrooms costs less than 20 mil everywhere and outperforms 12 mil everywhere. Most retailers will happily split an order across two products.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4: Is Vinyl Plank Flooring Waterproof? -->
        <div id="waterproof-reality" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Vinyl Plank Flooring Waterproof? Yes, With One Caveat
          </h2>

          <p>
            Unlike laminate, vinyl plank has no wood in the core, so the plank itself is genuinely unaffected by water. You can submerge a piece of SPC for a week and it will be unchanged. That is the property that made the category dominant in kitchens, bathrooms, and basements.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The planks are waterproof, the installation is not.</strong> Water that gets through the seams still reaches the subfloor beneath, where it can grow mould, damage plywood, or sit trapped for months. The floor survives; the structure under it may not. This is the distinction the marketing skips.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Floating floors trap water.</strong> Because a floating installation is not bonded to the subfloor, a spill that finds a seam has a large void to spread through and no way to evaporate. A leaking dishwasher can wet a substantial area before anyone notices anything at the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Glue-down is the answer in genuine wet areas.</strong> Full-spread adhesive removes the void, so water has nowhere to travel and the floor can be dried from above. In a commercial bathroom or a basement with a real flood history, glue-down vinyl is what professionals specify.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Silicone the perimeter in bathrooms.</strong> Sealing the expansion gap at the perimeter with flexible silicone instead of leaving it open behind the trim keeps water from running straight down the edge into the void. It preserves the ability to expand while blocking the most common entry point.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Basements still need a vapour barrier.</strong> A slab releases moisture upward continuously. Vinyl does not care, but that moisture will condense under the planks and can grow mould in the void. Over concrete, a barrier belongs under the floor regardless of what the planks are made of.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Waterproof Plank, Vulnerable Subfloor</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              If a supply line fails under a floating vinyl floor, the planks will be fine and the plywood beneath may not be. Vinyl buys you time and salvageable flooring, not immunity. In rooms with real flood risk — a laundry, a basement, a bathroom over a finished ceiling — glue-down installation and a siliconed perimeter are worth the extra effort.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: How to Install Vinyl Plank Flooring -->
        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Install Vinyl Plank Flooring Without the Common Failures
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rigid core vinyl is among the easiest floors to install, and the mistakes people make are predictable enough to list in advance.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Subfloor flatness is non-negotiable.</strong> Manufacturers typically specify flatness within 3/16 of an inch over 10 feet. Rigid planks bridge dips rather than following them, which means the floor rocks, the joints work, and eventually locking edges crack. Check with a long straightedge before ordering.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Leave the expansion gap even though it is plastic.</strong> Vinyl expands and contracts with temperature more than people expect, particularly in direct sun. A gap of roughly a quarter inch at every wall and fixed object, covered by trim, prevents the buckling that otherwise appears the first hot week.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Acclimate the boxes.</strong> Forty-eight hours flat in the room where they will be installed lets the planks reach the temperature they will live at. Laying cold planks in a warm room guarantees movement afterwards, and gapping at the seams is the usual result.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not pin the floor.</strong> A floating floor must slide, which means nothing can fasten through it — not transitions, not cabinet toe kicks, not a heavy island bolted down. Anything anchoring the field causes buckling elsewhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Stagger seams properly.</strong> End joints at least six inches apart, ideally more, and avoid a repeating stair pattern across the room. It costs nothing and it is the difference between a floor that looks laid and one that looks installed.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Watch for direct sunlight.</strong> Large south-facing glazing can heat a vinyl floor considerably, and while quality SPC is stable, extreme heat still causes movement. In a sunroom, confirm the product is rated for it and be generous with the expansion gap.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Vinyl Plank vs Laminate -->
        <div id="vs-laminate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Vinyl Plank vs Laminate
          </h2>

          <p>
            The two categories compete for the same rooms and the same budget. Neither is better overall; they fail in different places.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Vinyl Wins</h3>

          <p>
            Water, decisively and permanently. There is no wood in the core, so spills, humidity, and even standing water leave the plank unchanged. It is also softer and warmer underfoot than laminate, and quieter without needing special underlayment. That makes it the correct choice for bathrooms, laundries, kitchens, and basements.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Laminate Wins</h3>

          <p>
            Surface realism and hardness. A melamine wear layer over deep registered embossing produces texture that high-end laminate does better than most vinyl, and the harder surface resists denting from dropped objects and furniture legs more effectively than any WPC and most SPC.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Practical Split</h3>

          <p>
            Use vinyl anywhere water is plausible and anywhere comfort underfoot matters. Use laminate in living rooms, bedrooms, and hallways where realism and dent resistance matter more. Plenty of houses are best served by running both and matching them at the doorways.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cost Is Not the Deciding Factor</h3>

          <p>
            Quality examples of each land in a similar price band, and cheap examples of each disappoint in similar ways. Choose on room function rather than budget, because the money saved by putting the wrong product in a bathroom disappears the first time something leaks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: How to Clean Vinyl Plank Flooring -->
        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Clean Vinyl Plank Flooring and Keep It Looking New
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vinyl is genuinely low maintenance, and the small number of things that damage it are worth knowing because most of them are done with good intentions.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Grit is what actually wears the floor.</strong> Sand and dirt tracked in from outside act as abrasive under every footstep, and they do more damage to a wear layer over five years than any spill. Doormats at every exterior door and regular sweeping or vacuuming do more for longevity than any product you can buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Vacuum on the hard floor setting.</strong> A rotating beater bar designed for carpet can scuff a vinyl surface and, over time, dull the finish in traffic lanes. Most vacuums have a setting that lifts or stops the brush roll, and using it costs nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Damp mop, do not flood.</strong> Even on a genuinely waterproof floor, standing water finds seams and travels into the void beneath a floating installation. A well-wrung microfibre mop cleans as effectively as a wet one and keeps water out of the joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip steam mops entirely.</strong> Heat and pressurised moisture can soften the wear layer, weaken the adhesive on glue-down installations, and force water through seams. Most manufacturers explicitly void the warranty for steam cleaning, so check before assuming it is fine.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Avoid wax, polish, and oil soaps.</strong> Vinyl comes with a factory finish that needs no topping up, and wax or polish builds a film that dulls, attracts dirt, and is genuinely difficult to remove. A pH-neutral cleaner made for luxury vinyl is all it wants.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Protect against furniture and sunlight.</strong> Felt pads under every leg prevent the point-load denting that WPC in particular is vulnerable to, and wide non-staining floor protectors belong under heavy appliances. In rooms with strong direct sun, blinds or UV film prevent the gradual colour shift that affects any printed floor.
          </p>
        </div>

