        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best vinyl plank flooring?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                COREtec Plus Premium is the best overall, with a WPC core and attached cork that make it far warmer and quieter underfoot than rigid alternatives. Lifeproof Rigid Core is the value pick with better dent resistance, and Karndean Korlok Select has the most convincing visuals if budget allows.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between SPC and WPC vinyl?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                SPC uses a dense limestone and PVC core that is rigid, dent-resistant, and dimensionally stable, making it right for heavy furniture, sunrooms, and radiant heat. WPC uses a wood flour and PVC core that is thicker and softer, so it feels warmer and quieter underfoot but dents more readily. Both are equally waterproof.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What wear layer do I need for vinyl plank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Twelve mil is the sensible minimum for rooms people walk through daily. Step up to 20 mil for entryways, hallways, and homes with large dogs, where thinner layers develop a visible worn path. Six mil belongs only in bedrooms and low-traffic rooms. Wear layer predicts longevity far better than total plank thickness.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is vinyl plank flooring really waterproof?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The planks are, because there is no wood in the core. The installation is not: water reaching a seam on a floating floor travels into the void beneath and can damage the subfloor or grow mould while the planks stay perfectly fine. In genuine wet areas, glue-down installation and a siliconed perimeter solve this.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can vinyl plank go over concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is one of the best choices for a basement slab. Use a vapour barrier underneath regardless of the planks being waterproof, because slabs release moisture upward continuously and that moisture will condense in the void beneath a floating floor. Check the slab is flat within about 3/16 inch over 10 feet first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does vinyl plank flooring dent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                WPC cores dent noticeably under concentrated loads such as furniture legs, piano casters, or dropped tools, and furniture pads are genuinely necessary rather than advisable. Dense SPC cores resist denting far better, which is the main reason to choose them for a home with heavy furniture or a gym.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Vinyl Plank Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vinyl is forgiving to install and unforgiving about two things, both decided before the first plank goes down.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Thickness Instead of Wear Layer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Total millimetres affect how the floor feels and sounds; the wear layer in mils determines how long it looks new. An 8mm plank with a 6 mil layer wears out faster than a 5mm plank with 20 mil. Retailers advertise thickness because it sounds impressive, so find the wear layer in the specifications.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Waterproof Planks Mean a Waterproof Floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water reaching a seam on a floating installation spreads through the void underneath, wetting the subfloor and growing mould while the planks themselves stay perfect. In laundries, bathrooms, and basements, glue-down installation and a siliconed perimeter are what actually protect the structure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Subfloor Flatness Check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rigid planks bridge dips instead of following them, so an uneven subfloor produces a floor that rocks underfoot and locking edges that eventually crack. Manufacturers specify flatness within about 3/16 inch over 10 feet, and checking with a straightedge before ordering is far cheaper than lifting a finished floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting WPC Under Heavy Furniture Without Pads</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A WPC core is softer by design, and concentrated point loads from furniture legs, appliance feet, or a piano leave permanent impressions surprisingly quickly. Felt pads under everything are a requirement with WPC, not an optional refinement — or choose SPC instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

